ALBUKHR Contributor Admin — Read-Only Security Audit
Audit date: 21 October 2026
Scope: albu khr-admin (Main branch), albukhr-app (Main branch), albukhr-api (Main branch), Mainnet Supabase (ribpntyqdleytsyktdfb) and limited Testnet schema inventory (vhvkwvngmrlgyzwemttt).
Mode: Read-only. No files, database rows, policies, environment variables, deployments, or payment settings were changed.
Executive decision
Do not enable Contributor Admin appointment yet. ALBUKHR already has the right building blocks—an admin identity registry, role assignments, scoped bindings, MFA/AAL2, invitation tables and audit logging—but the current database does not expose a general admin-role invitation/grant/revoke workflow for the proposed Contributor Admin role. The existing Contributor invitation flow activates a Contributor; it does not appoint an administrator. The current Contributor onboarding page also contains no camera/selfie capture flow.
The implementation should extend the current Mainnet Admin Auth + albukhr_security authority, not add a second admin system or grant roles from frontend code.
Evidence-backed findings
1. Existing Contributor invitation flow is not an admin invitation flow
albukhr-admin/admin-contributor-invitations.html and js/admin-contributor-invitations.js call create_contributor_invitation, get_active_contributor_invitations, and revoke_contributor_invitation.
The page requires an active Mainnet Super Admin session and current AAL2 assurance.
Its invitation is consumed by the existing Pi-authenticated onboarding flow in albukhr-app/contributor-onboarding.html and js/contributor/contributor-onboarding.js, through the API routes /api/contributor/bootstrap, /api/contributor/consent, and /api/contributor/accept.
That page's steps are policy review, consent, and activation. The inspected files contain no getUserMedia camera capture or selfie-review workflow.
API src/contributor-gateway.js verifies Pi identity server-side and derives pi_uid from the verified token; the browser does not provide the authoritative UID. Preserve this pattern.
2. Mainnet has an admin RBAC foundation, but not a general Contributor Admin grant path
Observed in Mainnet Supabase:
albukhr_security.admin_users: 6 records, all currently active in the aggregate status count.
albukhr_security.admin_role_assignments: 6 active, unrevoked assignments — 1 super_admin and 5 core_admin.
Role enum values currently are super_admin, registry_admin, approval_admin, finance_admin, core_admin, internal_admin, and external_admin. There is no contributor_admin role yet.
albukhr_security.scoped_admin_bindings: 0 records at audit time.
albukhr_security.admin_invitation_links: 0 records.
The admin_invitation_links table exists, but the database function catalog scan found no albukhr_security function referencing that table. It should be treated as an unused/legacy structure until its original intent is confirmed—not as a working role invitation feature.
The only functions found inserting into admin_role_assignments are accept_core_project_invitation and accept_core_project_invitation_by_id; the inspected path grants core_admin with a Core Project binding. No general-purpose admin role grant/revoke RPC was found in the catalog scan.
get_my_admin_context() is the current server-authoritative path for returning status, active roles, project scope and Testnet access. Continue to use it as the source of truth.
3. Database authority is appropriately restricted; do not solve this with direct table writes
RLS is enabled on the relevant Mainnet admin tables.
Existing policies expose selected admin metadata to the authenticated owner or Super Admin; the audit log is Super-Admin-readable.
The authenticated and service-role database clients do not have direct table privileges for the checked admin tables in the inspected privilege snapshot. Current operations rely on carefully controlled SECURITY DEFINER RPCs owned by the database owner.
Therefore, new grant/revoke behavior must be delivered through dedicated, narrowly scoped RPCs that independently check the session, AAL2, active Super Admin role, role allowlist, target identity, scope, invitation state and audit requirements. Do not add broad table write policies or direct frontend table writes.
4. Contributor identity and Admin identity are different trust domains
At the audit snapshot:
The single Contributor row has an albukhr_user_id link but no auth.users link (contributors.user_id is null).
Admin authentication and role assignment are based on Supabase Auth auth.uid().
Contributor onboarding authenticates with Pi and resolves the authoritative public.users.pi_uid on the API side.
This is a material account-linking gap for this feature. A selected Contributor must not become an administrator simply because they possess an invitation URL, state an email, or submit a selfie. Acceptance must independently prove both (a) the nominated Pi identity and (b) the Supabase Auth identity used to sign into the Admin surface, and bind that proof to the same one-time nomination.
5. Selfie/camera and private-storage support are not present in the inspected flow
No selfie/biometric/identity-verification table was found in the relevant Mainnet schema inventory.
Mainnet currently has two Storage buckets in the inspected result: project-logos (public) and external-project-documents (private). No dedicated private selfie bucket was found.
Existing Storage object policies are specific to project logos and external project documents. Do not store admin selfies in the public logo bucket or reuse document access rules without a separate design and review.
A camera-only interface should use navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia() and capture from the active video stream, with no file/gallery picker. This ensures the page captures from an active camera stream, but does not by itself prove liveness or match the person to the account.
6. Testnet requires an additional compatibility check
The Testnet database contains public.testnet_admin_access_codes and public.testnet_admin_sessions. This audit did not inspect all application/runtime references to those tables, so their current operational role remains unconfirmed. Before implementation, confirm they are an access-gating mechanism consistent with Mainnet authority and do not establish a second independent source of admin roles. New Contributor Admin assignments should remain authoritative in Mainnet.
